INT. VICTORIAN CONSERVATORY, GLASS WINTER-GARDEN - 1887 - AFTERNOON

Palms press against the glass like prisoners. A fountain, dry. Wrought-iron chairs gone green with weather.

LADY CORDELIA WHITTINGTON-BANDELE (30s, bustled gown of dove gray, a face trained to give nothing away) sits with a teacup untasted in her gloved hands.

Across from her, MR. RUPERT ABERNETHY-ODUYA (40s, frock coat, whiskers meticulously combed) turns his hat in his fingers.

By the fountain's rim, partly hidden by a palm, stands MISS PHILIPPA LOVELACE (20s, governess' grey wool, spectacles), who has been told to wait and is waiting.

RUPERT
Lady Cordelia, I must speak plainly.

CORDELIA
Mr. Abernethy, you have never done otherwise, and I have never thanked you for it.

RUPERT
Then permit me to continue.

CORDELIA
Permit nothing. Say it.

He rises. She rises. Philippa shrinks behind the palm. Rupert crosses the tiles in two strides and Cordelia steps back until her skirts catch the iron chair. It scrapes. The three of them freeze, an arrangement of statues in a house of glass.

RUPERT
Miss Lovelace, you may go.

PHILIPPA
Yes, sir.

CORDELIA ^
No, Philippa. Stay.

RUPERT
Cordelia--

CORDELIA
Mr. Abernethy, whatever you have to say about the letters, you may say before my governess. She wrote them.

Philippa lifts her chin. The palm leaves stir, though there is no wind. Rupert's hat slips from his hand and rolls, round and round, to the dry fountain's edge.
